1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:04,040
On September 3, 2016, a
research vessel named Martin

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:06,540
Bergmann was heading north
through the Canadian Arctic.

3
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:10,040
Its crew was going to join
the search for HMS Terror,

4
00:00:10,520 --> 00:00:14,461
one of two ships that had disappeared
during an expedition led by John Franklin.

5
00:00:15,101 --> 00:00:17,601
The main search was taking
place much farther north.

6
00:00:18,221 --> 00:00:20,801
But Sammy Kogvik, a local
man aboard the vessel,

7
00:00:21,161 --> 00:00:24,661
had told another crew member about
something he had seen years earlier:

8
00:00:25,321 --> 00:00:29,441
a large wooden object rising out of
the ice in a bay called Terror Bay.

9
00:00:30,181 --> 00:00:33,141
It was an observation, not
proof that a ship lay below.

10
00:00:33,821 --> 00:00:36,661
The crew changed course anyway
and went to examine the bay.

11
00:00:37,481 --> 00:00:39,581
Erebus had been found two years earlier.

12
00:00:40,261 --> 00:00:41,681
Terror was still missing.

13
00:00:42,461 --> 00:00:46,422
Before Erebus was found, searchers knew
where Franklin's crews had abandoned

14
00:00:46,442 --> 00:00:49,162
the ships, but not where
either had finally sunk.

15
00:00:49,942 --> 00:00:53,742
In May 1845, Erebus and
Terror sailed from England

16
00:00:53,782 --> 00:00:55,502
to search for the Northwest Passage,

17
00:00:56,002 --> 00:00:59,442
a sea route through the Arctic connecting
the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans.

18
00:01:00,162 --> 00:01:04,782
There were 129 people aboard the two
ships, under Franklin's overall command.

19
00:01:05,542 --> 00:01:10,722
In September 1846, both vessels became
trapped in ice near King William Island.

20
00:01:11,423 --> 00:01:13,843
Franklin died in June 1847.

21
00:01:14,523 --> 00:01:18,003
By April 1848, the 105 surviving

22
00:01:18,043 --> 00:01:21,743
members of the expedition had abandoned
the ships and planned to travel south.

23
00:01:22,463 --> 00:01:25,483
They recorded those events in
a note left in a stone shelter.

24
00:01:26,143 --> 00:01:30,743
The note was written in 1848, but
searchers did not find it until 1859.

25
00:01:30,863 --> 00:01:35,563
It established where the ships had been
trapped and where the crews had left them.

26
00:01:36,243 --> 00:01:39,164
It did not establish where the
hulls finally came to rest.

27
00:01:39,944 --> 00:01:43,984
A ship abandoned in ice might not remain
at the place where its crew walked away.

28
00:01:44,704 --> 00:01:48,204
Anyone searching for Erebus and Terror
had to reckon with that difference.

29
00:01:48,944 --> 00:01:51,064
The crews' note was not the only evidence.

30
00:01:51,684 --> 00:01:54,624
Before it was found, Inuit
people living and travelling

31
00:01:54,644 --> 00:01:58,544
in the region had already shared accounts
of encounters connected to the expedition.

32
00:01:59,524 --> 00:02:03,544
In 1854, the explorer John Rae
received accounts from Inuit

33
00:02:03,584 --> 00:02:07,985
people about Europeans travelling south
and about the bodies of men found later.

34
00:02:08,645 --> 00:02:12,805
An Inuit interpreter and guide,
William Uligpak, also known as Mar-ko,

35
00:02:13,205 --> 00:02:15,805
played an important part in
communicating this information.

36
00:02:16,545 --> 00:02:19,605
Rae also obtained objects that
Inuit people had collected

37
00:02:19,805 --> 00:02:21,345
after the expedition disappeared.

38
00:02:22,065 --> 00:02:24,045
These reports did not locate a wreck.

39
00:02:24,345 --> 00:02:28,765
They did, however, describe events beyond
the position recorded in Franklin's note.

40
00:02:29,525 --> 00:02:32,065
Charles Hall later gathered
further Inuit accounts,

41
00:02:32,485 --> 00:02:35,685
working with the interpreters and
guides Takulittuk and Ipiivik.

42
00:02:36,586 --> 00:02:40,126
Some of the accounts described a ship
seen among the ice farther south,

43
00:02:40,566 --> 00:02:43,186
away from the place where the
crews had abandoned their ships.

44
00:02:43,966 --> 00:02:45,586
That ship was said to have sunk.

45
00:02:46,386 --> 00:02:50,966
The descriptions could not, by themselves,
identify it as Erebus or Terror.

46
00:02:51,586 --> 00:02:54,766
But they pointed toward a
different part of the coast.

47
00:02:54,886 --> 00:02:59,486
In 1869, an Inuit man named
Inukpudjijuk indicated an area

48
00:02:59,506 --> 00:03:01,846
to Hall on a map and made his own sketch.

49
00:03:02,566 --> 00:03:05,427
The area he indicated lay
near the Adelaide Peninsula,

50
00:03:05,747 --> 00:03:08,707
in a southern region known
in Inuit accounts as Ugyulik.

51
00:03:09,487 --> 00:03:11,647
Inukpudjijuk had not
witnessed the sinking,

52
00:03:11,987 --> 00:03:14,347
and his indication was
not exact coordinates.

53
00:03:14,887 --> 00:03:17,827
But it gave the accounts a
specific geographical reference.

54
00:03:18,507 --> 00:03:21,747
The 1848 note showed where
the crews had left the ships.

55
00:03:22,527 --> 00:03:25,947
Inuit accounts suggested a vessel
had later been seen farther south.

56
00:03:26,687 --> 00:03:27,947
Neither identified a wreck,

57
00:03:28,307 --> 00:03:30,967
but the southern coast had
become a place worth searching.

58
00:03:31,647 --> 00:03:34,348
For decades, searches found neither wreck.

59
00:03:35,028 --> 00:03:38,128
In the twentieth century,
David Woodman revisited Inuit

60
00:03:38,168 --> 00:03:41,048
testimony recorded by
nineteenth-century investigators.

61
00:03:41,668 --> 00:03:44,528
He compared descriptions of
ships, coastal landmarks,

62
00:03:44,748 --> 00:03:49,048
and encounters with Franklin's men, using
them to consider possible search areas.

63
00:03:49,808 --> 00:03:52,948
Woodman joined searches himself
but did not find the ships.

64
00:03:53,808 --> 00:03:58,348
In Gjoa Haven, Inuit historian Louie
Kamookak collected accounts from elders,

65
00:03:58,788 --> 00:04:02,209
studied local place names, and
related historical descriptions

66
00:04:02,269 --> 00:04:04,629
to the geography known by
the region's residents.

67
00:04:05,449 --> 00:04:07,449
Names written down by nineteenth-century

68
00:04:07,489 --> 00:04:09,989
visitors did not always
match modern charts.

69
00:04:10,749 --> 00:04:13,029
Understanding the places
named in old accounts

70
00:04:13,129 --> 00:04:16,809
required knowledge of the actual
coast, islands, and waters.

71
00:04:17,589 --> 00:04:20,429
Kamookak's work was rooted
in the region's own history,

72
00:04:20,889 --> 00:04:22,889
not simply in the search
for the British ships.

73
00:04:23,729 --> 00:04:28,829
Woodman and Kamookak joined a search in
1997 with Parks Canada and other partners.

74
00:04:29,550 --> 00:04:30,530
No ship was found.

75
00:04:31,210 --> 00:04:33,490
Parks Canada continued
working with Kamookak,

76
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:36,550
whose research drew on
accounts passed down by elders.

77
00:04:37,310 --> 00:04:40,550
Recorded testimony, local
knowledge, and field searches

78
00:04:40,610 --> 00:04:45,130
contributed in different ways; none
amounted to ready-made wreck coordinates.

79
00:04:45,890 --> 00:04:51,170
In 2008, underwater archaeologists from
Parks Canada began a multiyear search.

80
00:04:51,830 --> 00:04:54,010
They reviewed historical records and Inuit

81
00:04:54,030 --> 00:04:56,510
accounts and consulted
local knowledge holders.

82
00:04:56,890 --> 00:05:01,631
They identified two principal areas: one
near where the ships had been abandoned

83
00:05:01,671 --> 00:05:06,811
in 1848, and another farther south,
where Inuit accounts had placed a vessel.

84
00:05:07,511 --> 00:05:10,711
Parks Canada archaeologists
scanned the seabed with sonar,

85
00:05:11,091 --> 00:05:13,351
using sound to image underwater objects.

86
00:05:13,971 --> 00:05:17,191
Government of Nunavut archaeologists
examined sites on land,

87
00:05:17,451 --> 00:05:19,011
including former Inuit camps.

88
00:05:19,651 --> 00:05:22,651
Inuit knowledge holders
contributed history and geography.

89
00:05:23,231 --> 00:05:25,832
Coast guard and other survey
teams supported the work.

90
00:05:26,572 --> 00:05:29,052
Several seasons passed
without a confirmed wreck.

91
00:05:29,752 --> 00:05:34,392
Then, on September 1, 2014, a
Government of Nunavut archaeology

92
00:05:34,432 --> 00:05:37,552
team landed on a small island
in the southern search area.

93
00:05:38,072 --> 00:05:40,372
They were investigating
traces of an Inuit camp,

94
00:05:40,752 --> 00:05:43,572
including a stone outline where
a dwelling had once stood.

95
00:05:44,172 --> 00:05:46,512
During the survey, a pilot named Andrew

96
00:05:46,572 --> 00:05:49,392
Stirling noticed a rusted
piece of metal on the shore.

97
00:05:50,012 --> 00:05:52,312
Archaeologists examined it and recognized

98
00:05:52,392 --> 00:05:55,333
part of a ship's mechanism for
raising and lowering a boat.

99
00:05:55,873 --> 00:05:59,253
Its design corresponded to equipment
shown in drawings of Erebus.

100
00:05:59,913 --> 00:06:02,213
The team also found a wooden plug used

101
00:06:02,253 --> 00:06:04,633
to close a hole through
which a ship's rope passed.

102
00:06:05,213 --> 00:06:06,913
Neither object pinpointed a wreck,

103
00:06:07,273 --> 00:06:10,393
but both connected that stretch
of shore with shipboard equipment.

104
00:06:11,053 --> 00:06:14,453
Archaeologist Douglas Stenton
carried the finds to the icebreaker

105
00:06:14,533 --> 00:06:17,633
Sir Wilfrid Laurier, where members
of the search team were based.

106
00:06:18,213 --> 00:06:20,813
The following morning,
Parks Canada archaeologist

107
00:06:20,953 --> 00:06:23,294
Ryan Harris changed the
area to be surveyed.

108
00:06:23,814 --> 00:06:27,694
Instead of continuing the sonar work
as previously planned, the team moved

109
00:06:27,734 --> 00:06:31,494
its planned sonar survey closer to the
shore where the objects had been found.

110
00:06:32,014 --> 00:06:34,994
The research boat Investigator
began scanning the seabed.

111
00:06:35,514 --> 00:06:39,234
Within minutes, a large ship's
hull appeared on the sonar screen.

112
00:06:39,814 --> 00:06:42,734
After years of searches,
a wreck had been located.

113
00:06:43,314 --> 00:06:44,594
Underwater archaeologists

114
00:06:44,714 --> 00:06:47,894
examined its construction and
compared it with historical plans.

115
00:06:48,454 --> 00:06:51,335
They confirmed that the
vessel was HMS Erebus.

116
00:06:52,035 --> 00:06:57,355
On September 2, 2014, one of Franklin's
two missing ships had finally been found,

117
00:06:57,795 --> 00:07:00,375
in the southern search area
near the Adelaide Peninsula.

118
00:07:00,955 --> 00:07:04,815
The discovery did not come from exact
coordinates preserved in an old account.

119
00:07:05,355 --> 00:07:09,695
The team had studied a place on land,
recognized potentially relevant objects,

120
00:07:10,075 --> 00:07:11,815
and then shifted the search on the water.

121
00:07:12,415 --> 00:07:16,015
A previously broad search area
had produced a confirmed location.

122
00:07:16,595 --> 00:07:18,695
Terror, however, was still missing.

123
00:07:19,376 --> 00:07:22,536
In 2016, the main search
focused farther north,

124
00:07:22,916 --> 00:07:25,116
closer to where the crews
had abandoned the vessels.

125
00:07:25,496 --> 00:07:29,156
Martin Bergmann was heading there when
Kogvik's report prompted a detour.

126
00:07:29,876 --> 00:07:33,416
Kogvik had seen the object about
six years earlier on a hunting trip.

127
00:07:34,016 --> 00:07:36,396
His observation did not identify a ship.

128
00:07:37,016 --> 00:07:41,296
Other stories in Gjoa Haven had also
described an unknown vessel in Terror Bay.

129
00:07:41,916 --> 00:07:43,096
The Martin Bergmann crew,

130
00:07:43,356 --> 00:07:46,636
working with the Arctic Research
Foundation, chose to investigate.

131
00:07:47,256 --> 00:07:48,897
They surveyed the bay with sonar.

132
00:07:49,517 --> 00:07:51,657
On the seabed was the shape of a large,

133
00:07:51,837 --> 00:07:55,057
three-masted ship, sitting
nearly upright on its keel.

134
00:07:55,717 --> 00:07:58,717
It lay roughly a hundred kilometres
from the main search area.

135
00:07:59,377 --> 00:08:03,357
An underwater vehicle then provided views
of well-preserved parts of the wreck,

136
00:08:03,677 --> 00:08:05,237
including interior spaces.

137
00:08:05,857 --> 00:08:07,757
The images showed that a ship was there.

138
00:08:08,317 --> 00:08:10,077
They did not yet establish its name.

139
00:08:10,757 --> 00:08:14,517
The Arctic Research Foundation informed
Canadian authorities of the discovery.

140
00:08:15,117 --> 00:08:19,158
Parks Canada specialists came to Terror
Bay to examine it archaeologically.

141
00:08:19,718 --> 00:08:21,998
They studied underwater
images and compared

142
00:08:22,058 --> 00:08:24,098
visible features with
the historical plans,

143
00:08:24,478 --> 00:08:27,138
including details of the
steering equipment and the bow.

144
00:08:27,798 --> 00:08:30,158
Those features distinguished
the wreck from Erebus.

145
00:08:30,838 --> 00:08:34,578
On September 18, 2016,
Parks Canada formally

146
00:08:34,618 --> 00:08:38,258
confirmed that the ship found
in Terror Bay was HMS Terror.

147
00:08:38,958 --> 00:08:40,778
The discoveries followed different leads.

148
00:08:41,438 --> 00:08:46,099
In 2014, finds on shore changed
a sonar survey and led to Erebus.

149
00:08:46,739 --> 00:08:51,799
In 2016, Kogvik's report led a vessel to
a different bay and the wreck of Terror.

150
00:08:52,479 --> 00:08:57,119
In each case, a location was tested and
the evidence underwater was examined

151
00:08:57,199 --> 00:08:58,759
before a ship was identified.

152
00:08:59,479 --> 00:09:03,119
Erebus lay south of King William
Island, near the Adelaide Peninsula.

153
00:09:03,719 --> 00:09:06,659
Terror lay in Terror Bay,
beside King William Island.

154
00:09:07,279 --> 00:09:09,819
What had been uncertain
areas on a chart became

155
00:09:09,939 --> 00:09:14,180
two identified wreck sites,
found in 2014 and 2016.

156
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:18,120
More than a century and a half after
the crews abandoned their ships,

157
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:19,880
both locations were known.

158
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:23,700
The search for Franklin's two
missing vessels had reached its end:

159
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:26,240
Erebus and Terror had been found.

